From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 15:26:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Contact shadows you notice, no band at the water's edge, a
 path of moonlight and froth round wading legs

Tuning from the first films of the contact shadows (#262) and the water
(#265), every changed number commented with why:

- The contact shadows' default is now what lookao 2 was, which read as a
  soft yard-wide shade along a step, under a barrel and in a wall's
  crease, with no rims or halos: the grade's per-hour strengths doubled
  (GAIN acts before the occlusion's clamp, so raising it is not the x2
  that was filmed). lookao stays relative to the new default.
- The water's mirror fades out over 22% of the screen toward its edge
  (was 6%) and the ripples break the hand-over to the sky up, so no
  straight, lighter band shows near the camera where mirrored rays leave
  the screen.
- The moon's path: the water's moon is the sky's (moon_dir_white, north-
  west, 24 to 55 degrees up from 23:00 to midnight); the first night film
  faced away from it. Its glint is now a lane, not a lobe: a ripple glints
  where its mirrored ray comes within about 2.6 degrees of the moon's
  bearing, over a long run of elevation toward the horizon and a short one
  toward the camera, at strength 0.8, with a faint unbroken path under the
  glints. The calm night ripples break it into glints along a narrow lane,
  dark water either side. (Revised after the second night film, #270, take
  271: a broader, brighter lobe, sharpness 64 at 2.0, made a white blaze
  round the hero, 16% of the frame above 230/255, and a white fleck on
  every ripple across the lake. A test of that frame's geometry finds 4.8%
  of the water bright in the lane against 34.6% for the lobe, and nothing
  bright off the moon's line.)
- Froth round something standing in the water: a ring of six points on
  the surface finds the step the legs make in the bed behind it, and a
  lumpy froth spreads out from their outline, thinning over the ripples,
  where the shore's band drew a crisp, thin, bright oval (and a white
  ring round every reed base in the shallows). The shore's
  band is a little dimmer (0.75 to 0.6) and patchy with the ripples.

Revised after the third films (#270, take 273, and the laptop's take 275 on
#268):

- The moon's lane was one unbroken milky ribbon that widened and brightened
  toward the camera (its brightest, 254, at the bottom of the frame), and
  1.12's white ripple flecks covered the rest of the lake. The lane is now
  broken into glints (a sparkle on the surface whose tops alone shine, its
  mean far off so nothing crawls), dimmer at the moon's mirror point and
  brightest a little beyond it, cut short toward the camera (0.05 rad, was
  0.12) and faded out within 10 yd of it; its unbroken path is fainter
  (0.05, was 0.12). By night 1.12's flecks keep a fifth of their light off
  the lane.
- The water read a quarter brighter after worldLook 0 and back: the zone's
  grade started its 3 s ramp over on the switch, and the stills a second
  later caught the night grade a third of the way in. The ramp now follows
  the zone under the classic look too, so the grade comes back at once.
- The wading froth still drew a thin, crisp, pale oval round the shins and
  a line across the gap between the legs. It is strongest at a leg and
  fades out over a reach that wanders along the outline with a noise (0.3
  to 1.2 times 0.45 yd), cut into lumps by a finer noise, with none where
  legs lie on both sides of a pixel, and no shore band beside them; at most
  0.45, was 0.55.
- Golden hour facing the sun: the look's own god rays and bloom whitened
  the sky from the sun upward (the same with the painted sky off). The
  17:30 key's bloom is 0.30 (was 0.50) and rays 0.6 (was 0.9), and a sky
  pixel takes a third of the rays (RAY_SKY_SHARE 0.35): over open sky every
  march finds sky, so they were a glow, not shafts.
- looksky clouds 0 left one bright cloud. No cover now sets the field's
  threshold over its peak (it eases to the old line at a clear day's 0.1),
  so 0 is an empty sky.

Revised after the fourth films (#270, take 280):

- The wading froth was a crisp, unbroken oval round both knees that
  crossed between the legs, and a wide white ellipse at chest height
  wading deeper. A tap whose point on the surface lay behind the hero
  read his body above the water, nearer than the surface, as a step 0 yd
  under it, so the froth traced his whole outline against the water. A
  tap now counts only where the scene behind it is under the surface;
  the froth is softer-edged and at most 0.35 (was 0.45).
- The golden-hour glare was a white disc about 40% of the frame wide
  with a hard rim. It is 1.12's own sun glare, a quad about 80 degrees
  across facing the sun added in gamma, whose faint outer texels the
  look's grade and bloom lifted to white. Under the new look it is under
  half as wide (0.45) and a third as strong (0.35); worldLook 0 keeps
  1.12's.
- 1.12's moon flecks still covered the whole lake, dimmer. Off the moon's
  lane they are gone now (NIGHT_FLECKS 0, was 0.2).
- The one-frame see-through hero after a settings change and the 0.6 to
  2 s hitch each time worldLook came back on cannot be told apart
  without the real client, so the client now says what they are: for 3 s
  after worldLook or one of the look's switches changes, client.log
  names each frame over 50 ms with the materials, liquids, images and
  meshes made again, and each change in how many render pipelines are
  still compiling (a mesh whose pipeline is not ready is skipped that
  frame), then a summary ("[look switch]").

Revised after the fifth films (#270, take 291):

- The golden-hour glare was still a clipped white disc 36% of the frame
  wide. The 0.45 size and 0.35 strength did take effect (the share of the
  frame over 230 fell from 25-32% to 17.4%, and the disc was about 80% of
  the shrunken quad), but 1.12's glare quad is drawn into the frame
  before the look's grade, whose golden-hour contrast, warmth and bloom
  lifted the sky round the sun, and any glare on it, past white nearly
  to the quad's edge. Under the new look the quad is now hidden and the
  look's pass draws the glare after its grade, from the quad's own
  envelope (the sun on screen, behind no tree or cloud): a small core and
  a faint halo in the sun's tint, screened on so it never clips. New
  console command lookglare <strength 0-4> | classic | grade: classic
  draws 1.12's quad under the new look again, for the still that
  compares the two.
- The wading froth was still a crisp, dotted, unbroken ring at the neck
  standing deep, with a dotted arc trailing behind wading deeper. The dots
  were the taps' per-pixel turn; it now wanders smoothly over the surface.
  Round the step a slow, drifting noise keeps about half the ring as
  patches, and the froth thins as the water deepens (0.45 to 1.2 yd).
- The hitch: one frame of 0.5 to 1.4 s each time worldLook came back to 1,
  in which almost nothing was made again. Each slow frame's "[look
  switch]" lines now carry its timing spans: the main schedules cut at
  their boundaries and between Update's world stages, what was left for
  the render thread and the present, and the render world's last frame
  by its sets. The next film names the span that holds it.
- Clouds in the water showed as greying, not shapes. The mirror does
  sample the painted clouds, but each pixel's rippled ray reached a
  different patch of them, so they averaged out. The sky's mirror now
  takes a calmer surface than the near reflections (half the ripple near
  the camera, a fifth from 40 yd).

